Z||000000||geyser||09-09-2000||01:34 AM||||Just got back from a trip to San Diego from the SF Bay Area. Now I remember why I bought the Fiero; what a pleasure it was to drive such a long distance. The car performed flawlessly non stop, except for fuel and dip in the warm ocean water outside of Ventura. I like to think my mechanical ability had something to do with it, but I know better.<BR>87 GT||||reg||6
Z||000001||StuGood||09-09-2000||02:56 PM||||Fieros <B>are</B> great on long trips, aren't they?  Pretty good for a "little commuter car!"<P>Plus, they're great in town.  And for driving to work.  And errands.  Pretty much any excuse to drive them!  What aren't they good for?  Oh, that's right - trips to the lumber yard  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Reminds me - once I drove my Fiero out to the local building materials place, and I came out with about a 10-foot length of electrical conduit I needed.  Walked out to the parking lot, and just to be silly, opened the trunk of the Fiero, looked into the trunk, then looked at the ten-foot conduit, looked back in the trunk - and then put on my best "Uh-oh!" look.<P>There was a guy putting a bunch of lumber into his big pickup truck in the parking spot beside my car.  I looked at him and meekly asked, "Umm.. could I borrow your truck?"  He blinked, looked at the conduit, looked at my car, back at his pickup, and started to say, "Well, I suppose.."<P>I grinned, waved him off, told him I was just kidding, and thanked him.  Reached into my trunk for the tubing cutter I had brought, and started cutting the conduit into short pieces (I was going to cut them anyway)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. He laughed and took off.<P>The funny thing is, I believe the guy would have actually helped me if I had let him.  Thought I'd share this little story, to point out that there are still some really nice people out there.  <P><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||09-09-2000||06:27 PM||||You mean you weren't gonna hold it out the window like a knight holding a lance. (Riding his flying horse)||||reg||1
